        "SIGNALMENT": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "SIGNALMENT",
          "canonical_heading": "SIGNALMENT",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "subheading",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Species",
              "pdf_page": 585,
              "printed_page": 503,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Cat",
              "pdf_page": 585,
              "printed_page": 503,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "Species"
            },
            {
              "type": "subheading",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Breed Predilections",
              "pdf_page": 585,
              "printed_page": 503,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Can be seen in any breed; Siamese,",
              "pdf_page": 585,
              "printed_page": 503,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "Breed Predilections"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Abyssinian, Burmese, and Himalayan may be",
              "pdf_page": 585,
              "printed_page": 503,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "Breed Predilections"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "predisposed.",
              "pdf_page": 585,
              "printed_page": 503,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "Breed Predilections"
            },
            {
              "type": "subheading",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Mean Age and Range",
              "pdf_page": 585,
              "printed_page": 503,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Most common at 1–7 years of age.",
              "pdf_page": 585,
              "printed_page": 503,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "Mean Age and Range"
            }
          ]
        },
        "SIGNS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "SIGNS",
          "canonical_heading": "SIGNS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Episodes of twitching of skin over lumbar area, violent swishing of tail, vocalizing, and biting or",
              "pdf_page": 585,
              "printed_page": 503,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "licking of flank, pelvic region, or tail.",
              "pdf_page": 585,
              "printed_page": 503,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Pupils often dilate, and cats can become aggressive or appear agitated and run wildly about the",
              "pdf_page": 585,
              "printed_page": 503,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "environment.",
              "pdf_page": 585,
              "printed_page": 503,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Episodes are several seconds to several minutes in length; cats are typically normal between episodes, but may be intolerant of being",
              "pdf_page": 585,
              "printed_page": 503,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "stroked along the back.",
              "pdf_page": 585,
              "printed_page": 503,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "General physical examination often reveals no abnormalities aside from possible alopecia and broken hair over lumbar area from self-mutilation; many cats resent palpation of thoracolumbar musculature, and manipulation of area may elicit an episode; no neurologic deficits present.",
              "pdf_page": 585,
              "printed_page": 503,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "CAUSES": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "CAUSES",
          "content_blocks": []
        },
        "RISK FACTORS": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "RISK FACTORS",
          "content_blocks": []
        },
        "CAUSES & RISK FACTORS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "CAUSES & RISK FACTORS",
          "canonical_heading": "CAUSES & RISK FACTORS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Not known whether this syndrome is manifestation of underlying behavioral problem, dermatopathy, focal seizure disorder, or abnormal sensory state causing neuropathic pain or itch; speculated that cause is multifactorial, or that syndrome is not distinct entity with single cause, but rather can develop from variety of different factors.",
              "pdf_page": 585,
              "printed_page": 503,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Cats that tend to be nervous or hyperexcitable may be at increased risk; environmental stressors can serve as trigger.",
              "pdf_page": 585,
              "printed_page": 503,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    },
    "DIAGNOSIS": {
      "present": true,
      "status": "content",
      "content_blocks": [],
      "subsections": {
        "DIFFERENTIAL DIAGNOSIS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "DIFFERENTIAL DIAGNOSIS",
          "canonical_heading": "DIFFERENTIAL DIAGNOSIS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Dermatologic conditions causing pruritus— parasitic (e.g., flea,Notoedres,Cheyletiella), fungal (e.g., dermatophytosis), or allergic (e.g., parasitic, inhalant, dietary); evaluate for underlying dermatitis; skin scrapings and fungal",
              "pdf_page": 585,
              "printed_page": 503,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "cultures can help confirm diagnosis.",
              "pdf_page": 585,
              "printed_page": 503,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Diseases",
              "pdf_page": 585,
              "printed_page": 503,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "of spinal cord, nerve roots, epaxial muscles, or vertebral column causing pain—degenerative (intervertebral disc disease), inflammatory (discospondylitis, meningitis, myositis), neoplastic, or traumatic; spinal radiographs helpful in evaluating for abnormalities; further diagnostics (e.g., advanced imaging, serology for infectious agents, cerebrospinal fluid [CSF]",
              "pdf_page": 585,
              "printed_page": 503,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "analysis) may be necessary.",
              "pdf_page": 585,
              "printed_page": 503,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Forebrain disease causing behavioral changes and/or seizures— metabolic (hepatic encephalopathy), infectious/inflammatory (feline leukemia virus, feline immunodeficiency virus, feline infectious peritonitis, cryptococcosis, toxoplasmosis), neoplastic, vascular; complete diagnostic evaluation including bile acid tolerance, testing for infectious causes, CSF analysis, and brain",
              "pdf_page": 585,
              "printed_page": 503,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "imaging may be indicated.",
              "pdf_page": 585,
              "printed_page": 503,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Behavioral condition—compulsive disorder; exclusion",
              "pdf_page": 585,
              "printed_page": 503,
              "column": 2
            }
          ]
        },
        "CBC/BIOCHEMISTRY/URINALYSIS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "CBC/BIOCHEMISTRY/URINALYSIS",
          "canonical_heading": "CBC/BIOCHEMISTRY/URINALYSIS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Frequently normal.",
              "pdf_page": 585,
              "printed_page": 503,
              "column": 2
            }
          ]
        },
        "CBC/CHEMISTRY/URINALYSIS": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
